for c in sorted(cmd['commands'],key=lambda c:(-c['observed_count'],c['word'])):
 a(f"| {c['word']} | {c['observed_count']} | {', '.join(map(str,c['modal_tariffs']))} | {', '.join(map(str,c['observed_tariffs']))} | {c['paper_counts'].get('2',0)} / {c['paper_counts'].get('3',0)} |")
a('\nGlossary rows **not observed as a primary command**: '+', '.join(x['syllabus_term'] for x in cmd['syllabus_glossary_rows'] if x['listed_but_not_primary_observed'])+'. This is a primary-demand finding, not proof that none appears inside an instruction. For example, graph interpretation is tested even when the opening command is calculate.\n')
a('Observed commands absent as standalone glossary entries include complete, derive, express, justify, mark, measure, plan, plot, show and tick; choose is also observed. These may be used only with their demonstrated task type and tariff. **Draw** must be split into drawing a graph/diagram and drawing a conclusion. **Plan, 8 marks** is a practical investigation, not an eight-fact recall card. A modal tariff is evidence of a task family, not a universal tariff or an official AO assignment. The later framework must keep these contextual distinctions and cannot treat every occurrence of a verb anywhere in the stem as a new question.\n')
a('## 4. What a creditable answer looks like')
a('\nThese are original summaries of located marking architecture. Every row links to an actual demand and report page in `curriculum/answer-shapes.json`; source anchors there are internal audit material.\n')
a('| Task family | Located marking structure | Example report PDF page |\n|---|---|---:|\n| Calculation, 3 marks | Relation, valid substitution, result; other calculations have different intermediate credit. | 2021 p.487 |\n| Conditional definition/law, 2 marks | Both the main relation and its physical condition; a keyword alone is insufficient. | 2021 pp.487, 489 |\n| Vector construction, 4 marks | Valid vector arrangement, resultant, magnitude and direction. | 2021 p.489 |\n| Five classifications, 3 marks | Banded credit: five correct = 3, three/four = 2, two = 1. | 2024 p.658 |\n| Specific application, 2 marks | Relevant purpose for each radiation, rather than a vague organ/body reference. | 2024 p.661 |\n| Graph, 5 marks | Axes/units, scales, two plotting credits, fitted line. | 2024 p.666 |\n| Gradient, 4 marks | Visible triangle, matching substitution, slope value, axis-ratio unit. | 2024 p.666 |\n| Practical justification, 2 marks | Decision or classification plus a reason tied to the data or instrument. | 2024 p.666; 2025 p.659 |\n| Plan, 8 marks | Operational method plus the task-specific table/control/graph/conclusion requirements; splits differ by year. | 2021 p.496; 2022 p.578; 2023 p.674; 2024 p.668; 2025 p.659 |\n')
a('Coverage added before Step 4: **99/99 calculate, 16/16 define and 22/22 explain demands** now have individually checked marking-table records. This includes **25/25 three-mark calculations**. `assessment-evidence/marking-family-coverage.json` names each demand, page, extraction method, verbatim audit anchor and original marking interpretation. These are lead-agent table checks; no independent review is claimed.\n')
a('The three-mark calculation evidence has several patterns: equation/substitution/result (2021 P2 Q3(b), PDF p.487); two calculation marks plus a unit (2021 P2 Q5(b)(ii), p.488; 2022 P2 Q9(c)(ii), p.572; 2024 P2 Q1(b)(iii), p.658); intermediate frequency or circuit resistance (2022 P2 Q8(b), p.571; Q10(b), p.573); and explicit conversion or rounding credit (2025 P2 Q2(a)(ii), p.649; Q5(a)(i), p.651; Q1(b)(i), p.648). There is no single marking split for calculate.\n')
a('Definitions also differ: mass is one mark (2021 p.488), specific heat capacity has two conditions (2022 p.572), and latent heat must not be silently replaced with specific latent heat (2024 p.659). Explain demands range from a one-mark comparative reason (2025 p.649) to a three-stage particle explanation (2020 p.468). Where a table gives a combined statement without separate clause weights, the record preserves that uncertainty rather than inventing a per-clause split.\n')
a(f'There is no universal “one sentence per mark” rule. No official per-part AO split has been invented. **{s["records_with_credit"]}/622 demands carry entered credit evidence: 199 usable MC keys and {len(sh)} structured/practical marking entries. The other {622-s["records_with_credit"]} lack entered checked marking architecture.** These remaining demands are outside the three requested primary-command families. Before using their part-specific marking rules in a plan, read and record the relevant table. This is a marking-architecture register, not a complete numerical answer key.\n')
a('**Original wording check:** no diagnostic or answer-shape narrative matches 12 consecutive normalised words from the held reports; zero matches. Intentional verbatim audit anchors are excluded. See `review/original-wording-check.json`.\n')
a('Source quality matters: the 2024 refraction marking table prints 2.5° where its own expression gives about 22.5° (PDF p.661, image checked). The 2024 spring gradient is inverse stiffness; the 2025 volume–mass slope is inverse density. The 2021 practical table swaps the question paper’s two/one-mark distribution between adjacent parts. These are recorded in `assessment-evidence/source-issues.json`, separate from candidate errors.\n')
a('## 5. Where the marks fall')
